<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">2nd King Edward VII's Own Gurkha Rifles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sirmoor Rifles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 2nd King Edward VII's Own Gurkha Rifles (The Sirmoor Rifles) was a Gurkha regiment of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>, raised in 1815 at Nahan in Sirmur as the Sirmoor Battalion from soldiers who had served the Gurkha army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a>. It won fame defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Rao's house at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1857. In 1947 it passed to the British Army, and in 1994 it was merged into the Royal Gurkha Rifles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/2nd-king-edward-viis-own-gurkha-rifles.jpg" alt="2nd King Edward VII's Own Gurkha Rifles" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Crowthrower · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sirmoor2GR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sirmoor Battalion was the first Gurkha unit in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> to see action, in the 3rd Mahratta War in 1817. As the 8th (Sirmoor) Local Battalion it won its first battle honour at Bhurtpore in 1825, and in the First Sikh War it fought at Bhudaiwal, Aliwal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sobraon">Sobraon</a>; when the Sikhs seized its colour, a small party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a> led by a havildar cut its way into their lines and took it back.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Mutiny</a> the battalion stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. For its part at Hindu Rao's House it received the Queen's Truncheon, which replaced the colours it gave up on becoming a rifle regiment in 1858. It became the 2nd Gurkha Regiment in 1861 and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> as royal patron in 1876. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it served first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>; the 2nd Battalion went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the 1st to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, where it helped take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the 1st Battalion fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>, the 2nd was captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> and spent much of the war as prisoners of the Japanese, and the 3rd took part in the Chindit operations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1943. In the British Army after 1947 the regiment served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a> from 1948 to 1960 and then in Malaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, where its two battalions merged in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1947 the regiment's 4th Battalion passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> as the 5th Battalion, 8th Gurkha Rifles (Sirmoor Rifles), keeping the old title at the request of its first Indian commanding officer, Nisi Kanta Chatterji. It fought in the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1965, at the Fatwal Ridge, and in 1971, in the defence of Chamb-Akhnur.</p><p>The 2nd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a> were the only Gurkha regiment without a khukuri on the cap badge. Their red piping, adopted in 1858 from the British 60th Rifles through links forged at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and the red toorie on the cap marked them out. Their Victoria Crosses include those of Subedar Lalbahadur Thapa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in 1943 and Rifleman Bhanbhagta Gurung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “2nd King Edward VII's Own Gurkha Rifles (The Sirmoor Rifles)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British restored the dynasty in 1815 and raised the <b>Sirmoor Battalion</b> there from former Gurkha soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/state/sirmur-state">Sirmur State</a></li></ul></section>
